      "explanation": "Snowflake is right; the legacy number is wrong and it is the one finance has been looking at. dbo.usp_rollup_storage_daily buckets samples with CAST(DATEADD(hour, -4, event_ts_utc) AS date), a fixed US Eastern daylight offset, while every other fact (usage events, audit, invoices, dims) uses the UTC day the timestamps arrive in. The legacy 2026-09-30 row therefore takes the four hours 2026-10-01 00:00-03:59 UTC (October samples) and drops 2026-09-30 00:00-03:59 UTC; e.g. tenant 06164f4b: legacy peak 636141 MB is a sample at 2026-10-01 01:05 UTC, the UTC-day peak is 629150 MB. The offset is also an hour wrong every day US Eastern is on standard time. Size: sum of tenant peaks is 57,611,251 MB legacy vs 57,357,765 MB UTC on 2026-09-15 (legacy +0.44%) and 62,608,914 vs 62,419,115 MB on 2026-09-30 (+0.30%); 58 of 60 tenants differ in sample_count and/or peak_storage_mb, plan_id and included_bytes match. Because usp_rate_overage prices overage from this fact, legacy overage invoices have been computed from the shifted peak. The model defaults to the UTC day; dbt var legacy_storage_day_eastern_fixed_offset=true reproduces the legacy rows exactly (gate PASS on both dates with it on). Data owners (Finance + Billing) decide whether invoices switch to the UTC day at cutover."
